The implementation, executed:

```go
func binarySearch(arr []int, target int) int {
    lo, hi := 0, len(arr)-1
    for lo <= hi {
        mid := lo + (hi-lo)/2
        if arr[mid] == target {
            return mid
        }
        if arr[mid] < target {
            lo = mid + 1
        } else {
            hi = mid - 1
        }
    }
    return -1  // not found
}

// binarySearch([]int{1,3,5,7,9,11,13}, 7) → 3 — verified
// binarySearch([]int{1,3,5,7,9,11,13}, 8) → -1 — verified
```

The three details that matter:
1. mid := lo + (hi-lo)/2 — NOT (lo+hi)/2 (the overflow-safe form — in Go's int it won't overflow, but the form is the standard)
2. lo <= hi (the loop includes the single-element case — off-by-one is the classic binary search bug)
3. lo = mid + 1 / hi = mid - 1 (the +/-1 — mid is already checked; excluding it guarantees termination)

The invariant: the target, if present, is always in [lo, hi]. Each iteration either finds it or strictly shrinks the range — the loop must terminate.

The prerequisite: the array MUST be sorted. Binary search on unsorted data is a random walk — correct by accident sometimes, wrong always.

The Go-specific note: sort.Search(n, f) is the standard library's general form — search the smallest i where f(i) is true over a sorted predicate. For simple lookups, sort.SearchInts / sort.SearchStrings are the direct calls.
